`encodeEvent` dispatches via `_eventEncoders[event.runtimeType]!(event)` (and `_kindOf` via `_eventKinds[runtimeType]!`). The author's reply is acknowledged: the table shape follows the #558 precedent, several tables cannot be `const`, every lookup keeps its original default arm, and today the golden corpus in `test/wire/wire_protocol_test.dart` happens to drive every sealed kind through the tables. That makes the current tree correct — but the guarantee is still a manual lockstep: a new event kind added to the sealed hierarchy **compiles fine** and fails only when someone remembers to extend the fixtures, in production, with `Null check operator used on a null value`.

This diff adds ten test files and none of them (or any other) asserts table-key-set == sealed-subtype-set, so the previously suggested cheap guard remains open:

```dart
test('every sealed AgentEvent kind has an encoder and a kind string', () {
  final all = <AgentEvent>[ /* one literal instance per sealed subtype */ ];
  for (final e in all) {
    expect(_eventEncoders.containsKey(e.runtimeType), isTrue,
        reason: '${e.runtimeType} has no encoder');
    expect(_eventKinds.containsKey(e.runtimeType), isTrue,
        reason: '${e.runtimeType} has no kind string');
  }
});
```

One list literal shared by the encoder/kind/assistant-encoder/decoder assertions turns future kind additions into a named CI failure instead of a runtime crash. Keeping this at IMPORTANT (not blocking): the behavior as shipped is correct and covered.
