`encodeEvent` still dispatches via `_eventEncoders[event.runtimeType]!(event)` and `_kindOf` via `_eventKinds[runtimeType]!`. The author's defense stands (the #558 precedent, non-const tables, default arms preserved, and today's golden corpus drives every sealed kind through the tables) — the tree as shipped is correct and covered. But this round's new commits add ~700 lines of tests elsewhere and still no assertion that the table key set equals the sealed-subtype set, so a future event kind keeps compiling clean and failing in production with `Null check operator used on a null value` until fixtures are manually extended.

The cheap guard remains a one-literal-per-kind list:

```dart
test('every sealed AgentEvent kind has an encoder and a kind string', () {
  final all = <AgentEvent>[ /* one literal instance per sealed subtype */ ];
  for (final e in all) {
    expect(_eventEncoders.containsKey(e.runtimeType), isTrue,
        reason: '${e.runtimeType} has no encoder');
    expect(_eventKinds.containsKey(e.runtimeType), isTrue,
        reason: '${e.runtimeType} has no kind string');
  }
});
```

Kept at IMPORTANT (not blocking): the shipped behavior is correct; this is a guard against the next contributor, not a defect in this diff. Same trade note applies to `HostCapability.id`'s `_ids[this]!` and `_isCommitEvent`'s collapsed default, both pinned by existing suites today.

